		bodyHtml: `<p>To forsake someone is to <strong>leave them behind: to abandon, desert or give up on a person you were bound to stay with</strong>. It is a heavier word than &ldquo;leave.&rdquo; You can leave a room; you forsake a child, a covenant, a people. The Bible uses it two ways. Sometimes it is a sin against our Father, when His people turn their backs on Him. More often, and more tenderly, it is the fear of the abandoned: the cry of someone who feels left alone.</p><p>It can also mean giving something up on purpose. Isaiah calls the wicked to &ldquo;abandon his way&rdquo; and promises that &ldquo;our Father&rsquo;s heart will be moved with compassion for him.&rdquo; Abandoning sin is repentance; being forsaken is the thing the Bible promises will never happen to you.</p>`,
